                  %path{:d => "M 14.541391 -64.661459 L 69.756624 -64.661459 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:butt;stroke-linejoin:miter;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.996597,0,0,-0.996597,106.496372,78.550761)"}
                  %path{:d => "M -2.48741 2.869839 C -2.032737 1.14914 -1.021484 0.333866 0.0015286 0.000701109 C -1.021484 -0.336383 -2.032737 -1.147738 -2.48741 -2.868437 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.996597,0,0,-0.996597,176.252383,142.992886)"}
            %p
              It immediately follows from the above unfolding of the definition that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} VBun
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} VBun
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \VBun(e'',e')\cong\VBun(e'',p)
              naturally in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo ″
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e''
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is the pullback bundle of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_{2}\longrightarrow M_{2}
              along
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{1}\longrightarrow M_{2}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma#YonedaCorollaries"} Yoneda lemma
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e'\cong p
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi e
                      %mo ′
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_{1}\cong\mathcal{P}(e')^{*}V_{2}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e'\cong p
              preserve the commutativity of the diagrams we have drawn above by the universal property of the pullback, and by the definition of a cartesian morphism. Now that we have identified the cartesian morphisms, the statement that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} VBun
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Man
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}:\VBun\longrightarrow\Man
              is a Grothendieck fibration is equivalent to the statement that vector bundles can be pulled back along smooth maps. This is of course true, and thus
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} VBun
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Man
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}:\VBun\longrightarrow\Man
              is a Grothendieck fibration. The associated functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{*}
              are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback#PullbackFunctor"} pullback pseudofunctors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} VBun
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi M
                          %mn 2
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} VBun
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi M
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{*}:\VBun_{M_{2}}\longrightarrow \VBun_{M_{1}}
              that pullback vector bundles over a manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{2}
              to vector bundles over a manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{1}
              along a smooth map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:M_{1}\longrightarrow M_{2}
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Let
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ring"} Ring
              be the category of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} rings
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              the category of pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R,M)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a ring and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a (left)
              = succeed "-module." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mod
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ring
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod\to Ring
              is a fibration and an opfibration. The functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
              are given by restriction of scalars,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
              is extension of scalars, and the right adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
              is coextension of scalars.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be any category with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free-living"} free-living
              arrow, so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\mathbf{2}}
              is the category of arrows and commutative squares in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\mathbf{2}} \to C
              is a fibration and opfibration. The cartesian arrows are precisely the pullback squares, and the functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
              are just given by composition. The right adjoints
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
              exist iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
          #SimpleFibration.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a category with binary
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+products"} cartesian products
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi Comonad
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to Comonad
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreader+comonad"} environment comonad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \times -
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+categories"} Kleisli categories
              we get a functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \to Cat
              %span.newWikiWord
                simple fibration
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/simple+fibration"} ?
              (e.g., in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Jacobs98"} Jacobs 1998
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              because it can be used to give semantics to simply-typed
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+calculus"} lambda calculus
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projections"} projections
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \times A \to \Gamma
          %p
          #FreeCoproductCompletion.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/families"}> families
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+coproduct+completion"} free coproduct completion
              %br/
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be any category and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Fam
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fam(C)
              be the category of set-indexed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/families"} families
              of objects of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              (the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+coproduct+completion"} free coproduct completion
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
              by the discussion
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "free+coproduct+completion#AsAGrothendieckConstruction"} there
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Fam
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fam(C)\to Set
              taking a family to its indexing set is a fibration; the functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
              are given by reindexing. They have left adjoints iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              has small coproducts, and right adjoints iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              has small products.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+extension"} group extension
              of a group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              by a group
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %mi G
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to K \overset{i}{\to} G \overset{p}{\to} H \to 1,
            %p
              can be viewed as a fibration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: G \to H
              between one-object categories, with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              playing the role of fiber. In selecting a cleavage, effectively a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo :
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s: H \to G
              between the underlying sets, one is led to a pseudofunctor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \to Cat
            %p
              that takes the single object of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              to the category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              to inner automorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi h
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^\ast
              (qua functors) on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              by conjugation
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi h
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mi k
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \mapsto s(h)^{-1}k s(h)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo :
                      %mi H
                      %mo →
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi: H \to Aut(K)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi h
                      %mn 2
                      %mo *
                    %msubsup
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_2^\ast h_1^\ast \cong (h_1 h_2)^\ast
              are tracked by (nonabelian) group 2-cocycle data
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo :
                    %mi H
                    %mo ×
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi: H \times H \to K: (h_1, h_2) \mapsto s(h_1 h_2)^{-1} s(h_1)s(h_2),
            %p
              as described
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "group+extension#SchreierTheoryTraditional"} here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              from the data
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo :
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi Aut
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi: H \to Aut(K)
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi χ
                      %mo :
                      %mi H
                      %mo ×
                      %mi H
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi: H \times H \to K
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/778/grothendieck-fibration/?Focus=123242#Comment_123242"} here
              for further related discussion.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                It is easy to verify that fibrations are closed under
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %li
              %p
                Every fibration (or opfibration) is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Conduch%C3%A9+functor"} Conduché functor
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiable+morphism"} exponentiable morphism
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %li
              %p
                Every fibration or opfibration is an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-limit"} strict 2-pullbacks
                of fibrations are also
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-pullbacks"} 2-pullbacks
            %li#FiberedLimit
              %p
                Suppose that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon A\to B
                is a fibration, and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                has
                = succeed "-indexed" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                has
                = succeed "-indexed" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                strictly preserves them if and only if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                has
                = succeed "-indexed" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+limit"} fibred limits
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "#Gray"} Gray 66
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Hermida"} Hermida 99, Corollary 4.9
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi I
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon I\to A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                be the limit of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi I
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p f\colon I\to B
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi L
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_i\colon L \to p(f(i))
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in I
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                        %msup
                          %mi p
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(i) = \phi_i^*(f(i)) \in p^{-1}(L)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi I
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon I\to p^{-1}(L)
                whose limit is the limit of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %li
              %p
                Dually, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon A\to B
                is an opfibration, then
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                can be constructed out of those in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                and in the fiber categories.
            %li
              %p
                Suppose that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon A\to B
                is a fibred
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
                with
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    simple products
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/simple+products"} ?
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                is cartesian closed, so is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                preserves the structure. (See
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a{:href => "#Hermida"} Hermida 99, Corollary 4.12
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#HermidaThesis"} Hermida’s thesis
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon A\to B
                is a fibration and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                admits an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system"} orthogonal factorization system
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo ,
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,M)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E',M')
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M'
                is the class of cartesian arrows whose image in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                lies in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E'
                is the class of all arrows whose image in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                lies in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                is an opfibration. If it is a bifibration (or more generally, an
                %span.newWikiWord
                  ambifibration
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/ambifibration"} ?
                over
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo ,
                        %mi M
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,M)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ternary+factorization+system"} ternary factorization system
            %li
              %p
                Under suitable hypotheses, versions of the preceding fact can be shown
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+systems"} weak factorization systems
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures"} model structures
                as well.
            %li
              %p
                Generalizing in a different direction, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon A\to B
                is a fibration and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,M)
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+structure+for+sinks"} factorization structure for sinks
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                admits a factorization structure for sinks
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mi M
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E',M')
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M'
                is the class of cartesian arrows whose image in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                lies in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E'
                is the class of all arrows whose image in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                lies in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %em cosinks
                along an opfibration. To lift in the “opposite” way we require more of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+concrete+category"} topological concrete category
          %h2#variations Variations
          %h3#discrete_and_groupoidal_fibrations Discrete and groupoidal fibrations
          %p
            One important special case of a fibration is when each fiber is a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^{op}\to Grpd
            = succeed "." do
              %em categories fibered in groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to B
            is fibered in groupoids precisely when
            %em every
            morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is cartesian.
          %p
            Another important special case is when each fiber is a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^{op}\to Set
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibrations"} discrete fibrations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon E\to B
            is a discrete fibration precisely when for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\in E
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi b
                  %mo →
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon b\to p(e)
            there is a
            %em unique
            lifting of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to a morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e'\to e
          %h3#opfibrations_and_bifibrations Opfibrations and bifibrations
          %p
            We say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon E\to B
            is an
            %strong opfibration
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi op
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{op}:E^{op}\to B^{op}
            is a fibration. These correspond to covariant pseudofunctors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\to Cat
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bifibration"} bifibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            has a left adjoint, written
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            also has a right adjoint, written
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_f
          %p
            Grothendieck originally called an opfibration a
            = succeed "," do
              %em cofibered category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+cofibered+in+groupoids"} category cofibered in groupoids
            (cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA+I"} SGA I
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
            %em lifting
            property, as is characteristic of other notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
            %em extension
            property exhibited by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            Note that an opfibration is the same as an internal fibration in the 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi co
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat^{co}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Cat
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat^{op}
            which are more deserving of the name “cofibration.”
          %p
            Note also that a given pseudofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^{op}\to Cat
            can be represented both by a fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_1\to B
            and by an opfibration
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_2\to B^{op}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_2
            is
            %em not
            the opposite category of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_1
          %h3#StreetFibration Version respecting the Principle of equivalence
          %p
            There is something apparently in violation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            about the notion of fibration, namely the requirement that for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:a\to b
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\in E_b
            there exists a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi e
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:e'\to e
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(e')
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+fiber"} essential fiber
          %p
            Note that almost any fibration between “concrete” categories that arises in practice does satisfy this strict property. However, even stating the strict property requires our categories to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            (i.e. to have a notion of equality of objects), so when working in a context where not all categories are strict (such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories+in+homotopy+type+theory"} internal categories in homotopy type theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/displayed+categories"} displayed categories
            instead, but in some cases (such as internally in a bicategory) one does not have classifying objects either, so it is sometimes useful to have a version which manifestly accords to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %p
            The correct modification, first given by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:a\to b
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\in E_b
            there exists a cartesian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi e
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:e'\to e
            and an
            %em isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:p(e') \cong a
            such that
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ h = p(\phi)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Street+fibration"} Street fibration
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            is a Street fibration, which is not true for the concept of Grothendieck fibrations according to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %p
            We might also think that it violates the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            to say that the target of the cartesian arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is equal to the given object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibrations"} Serre fibrations
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold+fibrations"} Dold fibrations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            of two objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B\to A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B \to B
            are
            %em equal
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon a\to b
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\in E_b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi e
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon e' \to \hat{e}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(\phi)=f
            and an isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{e}\cong e
            in the fiber
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            with the isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{e}\cong e
            to get a cartesian arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi e
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{\phi}\colon e'\to e
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(\phi)=f
            still. The reason this doesn’t work in topology is that paths come with parametrizations, and requiring the lower triangle (in the square drawn at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold+fibration"} Dold fibration
          %p
            The idea of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proto-fibration"} proto-fibration
            is closely related to this.
          %h3#internal_version Internal version
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:E\to B
            is called a fibration if for every object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X,E)\to K(X,B)
            is a fibration of categories, and for every morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:Y\to X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ,
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ K(X,E) &amp; \to &amp; K(Y,E) \\ \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow \\ K(X,B) &amp; \to &amp; K(Y,B)}
          %p
            is a morphism of fibrations. There is an alternate characterization in terms of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+object"} comma object
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+in+a+2-category"} fibration in a 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            above. Interpreted in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            we obtain the explicit notion we started with.
          %h3#twosided_version Two-sided version
          %p
            A
            = succeed "," do
              %strong two-sided fibration
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                fibration from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ←
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \leftarrow E \rightarrow B
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to A
            is a fibration,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to B
            is an opfibration, and the structure “commutes” in a natural way. Such two-sided fibrations correspond to pseudofunctors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{op}\times B \to Cat
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{op}\times B\to Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-sided+fibration"} two-sided fibration
            for more details.
          %p
            Note that a pseudofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{op}\times B \to Cat
            can also be represented by an opfibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_1\to A^{op}\times B
            and by a fibration
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_2\to A\times B^{op}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_2
          %h3#higher_categorical_versions Higher categorical versions
          %p
            There is also a notion of fibration for 2-categories that has been studied by Hermida. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fibration"} n-fibration
            for a general version.
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-categories
            the notion of fibered category is modeled by the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration"} Cartesian fibration
            of simplicial sets. The corresponding analog of the Grothendieck construction is discussed at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Grothendieck+construction"} (∞,1)-Grothendieck construction
          %h3#versions_for_other_categorical_structures Versions for other categorical structures
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                There is an analog for
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategories"} multicategories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+of+multicategories"} fibration of multicategories
            %li
              %p
                For higher multicategories, see
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration+of+dendroidal+sets"} Cartesian fibration of dendroidal sets
            %li
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+fibration"} double fibration
          %h2#alternate_definitions Alternate definitions
          %p
            There are several alternate characterizations of when a functor is a fibration, some of which are more convenient for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+in+a+2-category"} internalization
          %h3#in_terms_of_adjoints In terms of adjoints
          %p
            Since Grothendieck fibrations are a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            notion, in what follows we denote by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\downarrow p
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
            (i.e. determined up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat/B
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+slice+2-category"} strict slice 2-category
          .un_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon E \to B
              is a cloven fibration if and only if the canonical functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon E \to B\downarrow p
              has a right adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat / B
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              First, recall that the strict slice 2-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat/X
              has objects the functors
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to X
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi h
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                            %mo ′
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                            %mo ↘
                            %mo ↙
                            %mi g
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{C &amp; \stackrel{h}{\to} &amp; C' \\ &amp; f \searrow \swarrow g &amp;  \\  &amp; X,  &amp; }
            %p
              and as 2-cells the natural transformations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : h_1 \to h_2
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mi α
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\alpha = id_f
            %p
              Next, recall that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\downarrow p
              has objects the triples
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi e
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, e, k)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi x
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \colon x \to p e
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi p
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \colon B\downarrow p \to B
              denote the projection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi e
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, e, k) \mapsto x
            %p
              The canonical morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:E \to B\downarrow p
              is simply the inclusion functor of identity maps
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi e
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mi e
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi p
                      %mi e
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i e  = 1_{p e} \colon p e \to p e
            %p
              Somewhat imprecisely, seeing both categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\downarrow p
              as sitting over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              means that functors between those should be the identity on the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              component, and natural transformations should have the identity as their
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              component.
            %p
              To give an adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \dashv r
              it suffices to give, for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \colon x \to p e
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\downarrow p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r k
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mi r
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p r k = x
              and an arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mi r
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i r k = 1_x \to k
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\downarrow p
              that is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+arrow"} universal
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat / B
              we must have that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi i
                      %mi r
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mi r
                          %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \circ i r k = 1_{p r k} = 1_x
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi p
                                %msub
                                  %mi ϵ
                                  %mi k
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi k
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    x &amp; \overset{1}{\to} &amp; x \\
                    \\mathllap{1} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{p \epsilon_k} \\
                    x &amp; \underset{k}{\to} &amp; p e
                    }
            %p
              and thus amounts to a choice of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi r
                    %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_k \colon r k \to e
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %msub
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \epsilon_k = k
            %p
              The universal property of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_k
              tells us that for any other morphism in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\downarrow p
              from some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i y
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              and any pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g)
              making the square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mi y
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mi y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi p
                                %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi k
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    p y &amp; \stackrel{1}{\to} &amp; p y \\
                    \\mathllap{f} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{p g} \\
                    x &amp; \underset{k}{\to} &amp; p e
                    }
            %p
              commute, there is a unique map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi y
                    %mo →
                    %mi r
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \colon y \to r k
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              such that the above square factors in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\downarrow p
              as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mi y
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mi y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi p
                                %mi h
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi p
                                %mi h
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi p
                                %mi r
                                %mi k
                                %mo =
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo =
                                %mi p
                                %mi r
                                %mi k
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi p
                                %msub
                                  %mi ϵ
                                  %mi k
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi k
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mi e
                            %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    p y &amp; \stackrel{1}{\to} &amp; p y \\
                    \\mathllap{p h} \downarrow &amp;  &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{p h} \\
                    \\mathllap{p r k =} x &amp; \stackrel{1}{\to} &amp; x \mathrlap{= p r k}\\
                    \\mathllap{1} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{p \epsilon_k} \\
                    x &amp; \underset{k}{\to} &amp; p e.
                    }
            %p
              In other words, the universal property provides a unique
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi k
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_k h = g
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mi h
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p h = f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_k
              is a cartesian lift of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %p
              So the existence of a right adjoint to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              means precisely that for each morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \colon x \to p e
              a choice is given of a cartesian lift of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a cloven fibration.
          %h3#in_terms_of_pseudoalgebras In terms of pseudoalgebras
          %p
            Fibrations are pseudoalgebras for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+pseudomonad"} lax-idempotent pseudomonad
            (see there for more details).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opfibration"}> opfibration
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bifibration"} bifibration
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-sided+fibration"} two-sided fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+fibration"}> small fibration
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+fibration"} locally small fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+fibration"} monoidal fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+of+2-categories"} fibration of 2-categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fibration"} n-fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration"} Cartesian fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliated+category"} foliated category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"}> Grothendieck construction
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
          %h2#References References
          %p
            The concept was introduced in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent+theory"} descent theory
            and in the guise now known as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+categories"} indexed categories
          %ul
            %li#Grothendieck60
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              , p. 300 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Technique de descente et théorèmes d’existence en géométrie algébrique. I. Généralités. Descente par morphismes fidèlement plats
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N.+Bourbaki"} N. Bourbaki
              exp. no190 (1960) 299-327 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=SB_1958-1960__5__299_0"}> numdam:SB_1958-1960__5__299_0
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p and then elaborated on in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              , exposé VI of:
              %em Revêtements Etales et Groupe Fondamental - Séminaire de Géometrie Algébrique du Bois Marie 1960/61
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA+1"}> SGA 1
              ) , LNM
              %strong 224
              Springer (1971) &amp;lbrack;updated version with comments by M. Raynaud:
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0206203"} arxiv.0206203
          %p Another important early reference:
          %ul
            %li#Gray
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+W.+Gray"}> John W. Gray
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fibred and Cofibred Categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"} S. Eilenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D.+K.+Harrison"} D. K. Harrison
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S.+MacLane"} S. MacLane
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H.+R%C3%B6hrl"} H. Röhrl
              (eds.):
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proceedings+of+the+Conference+on+Categorical+Algebra+-+La+Jolla+1965"} Proceedings of the Conference on Categorical Algebra - La Jolla 1965
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-99902-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-99902-4
          %p Early development of the theory:
          %ul
            %li{:id => "Bénabou1975"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"}> Jean Bénabou
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fibrations petites et localement petites
                %strong 281
                Série A (1975) 897-900 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/bpt6k6228235m/f171.image#"}> gallica
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li{:id => "Bénabou1985"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+B%C3%A9nabou"}> Jean Bénabou
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fibered Categories and the Foundations of Naive Category Theory
                %strong 50
                1 (1985) 10-37 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/2273784"}> doi:10.2307/2273784
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p reviewed in:
          %ul
            %li#Streicher18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Streicher"}> Thomas Streicher
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fibred Categories à la Jean Bénabou
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.02927"} arXiv:1801.02927
          %p
            Further review with emphasis on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            and Grothendieck fibrations as incarnations of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Angelo+Vistoli"}> Angelo Vistoli
                ,
                %em Grothendieck topologies, fibered categories and descent theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0412512"}> math.AG/0412512
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2223406"} MR2223406
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Fundamental algebraic geometry. Grothendieck’s
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FGA+explained"} FGA explained
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 123
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-123-s"} ISBN:978-0-8218-4245-4
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2007f:14001"} MR2007f:14001
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barbara+Fantechi"}> Barbara Fantechi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lothar+G%C3%B6ttsche"} Lothar Göttsche
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luc+Illusie"} Luc Illusie
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+L.+Kleiman"} Steven L. Kleiman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nitin+Nitsure"} Nitin Nitsure
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Angelo+Vistoli"} Angelo Vistoli
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Fundamental algebraic geometry. Grothendieck’s
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FGA+explained"} FGA explained
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 123
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2007f:14001"} MR2007f:14001
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-123-s"} ISBN:978-0-8218-4245-4
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://indico.ictp.it/event/a0255/other-view?view=ictptimetable"} lecture notes
            %li#Warner12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Garth+Warner"}> Garth Warner
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fibrations and Sheaves
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://hdl.handle.net/1773/20977"} hdl:1773/20977
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://sites.math.washington.edu//~warner/Warner_FIBRATIONS%20AND%20SHEAVES.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Warner-FibrationsAndSheaves.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Wells"} Charles Wells
                %em Category Theory for Computing Science
                , Prentice Hall 1995³. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/22/tr22abs.html"}> TAC reprints no.22 (2012)
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , Chapter 8 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                    vol. 2
            %li#Jacobs98
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"}> Bart Jacobs
                , Chapters 1 and 9 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical Logic and Type Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 141
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/bookseries/studies-in-logic-and-the-foundations-of-mathematics/vol/141"} ISBN:978-0-444-50170-7
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://people.mpi-sws.org/~dreyer/courses/catlogic/jacobs.pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (in the context of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics+of+dependent+types"} categorical semantics of dependent types
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
                  vol.1, Oxford UP 2002. (Part B)
              %blockquote
                %p (beware that this uses the non-standard terms “prone” and “supine” for “cartesian” and “opcartesian” morphisms)
            %li#JohnsonYau20
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niles+Johnson"}> Niles Johnson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Donald+Yau"} Donald Yau
                = succeed "," do
                  %em 2-Dimensional Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/2002.06055"} arXiv:2002.06055
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://oxford.universitypressscholarship.com/view/10.1093/oso/9780198871378.001.0001/oso-9780198871378"} doi:10.1093/oso/9780198871378.001.0001
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                André Joyal,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/joyalscatlab/published/Grothendieck+fibrations"} Grothendieck fibrations
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibred_category"} Fibered category
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of fibrations and application to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
          %ul
            %li#Hermida
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Hermida"}> Claudio Hermida
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Some properties of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Fib
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fib
                    as a fibred 2-category
                %strong 134
                (1999) 83-109 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-4049(97)00129-1"}> doi:10.1016/S0022-4049(97)00129-1
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#HermidaThesis
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Hermida"}> Claudio Hermida
                ,
                %a{:href => "https://era.ed.ac.uk/bitstream/handle/1842/14057/Hermida1993.Pdf"} PhD thesis, University of Edinburgh
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-comonad"} 2-comonad
            characterizing Grothendieck fibrations:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacopo+Emmenegger"}> Jacopo Emmenegger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luca+Mesiti"} Luca Mesiti
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giuseppe+Rosolini"} Giuseppe Rosolini
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Streicher"} Thomas Streicher
              %em A comonad for Grothendieck fibrations
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.01474"}> arXiv:2305.01474
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On the connection between Grothendieck fibrations and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorisation+systems"} factorisation systems
            (see also
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+factorisation+system"} stable factorisation system
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                J. Hughes and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"} Bart Jacobs
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Factorization systems and fibrations: Toward a fibred Birkhoff variety theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Rosick%C3%BD"}> Jiří Rosický
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Tholen"} Walter Tholen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Factorization, fibration and torsion
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0801.0063"} arxiv/0801.0063
            %li
              %p
                Miloslav Štěpán,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Factorization systems and double categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.06714"} arXiv:2305.06714
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> R. Brown
              , R. Sivera,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic colimit calculations in homotopy theory using fibred and cofibred categories
              %strong 22
              (2009) pp.222-251. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/22/8/22-08.pdf"}> pdf
              )
